    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "d8d97704-024a-3117-a73a-608c16a75928",
                "word": "Xanthippe",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "6a769dc6-b50a-48b9-92ed-62a5aaaf0e60",
                        "entry_id": "d8d97704-024a-3117-a73a-608c16a75928",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "Xanthippes"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "259ff65e-ea0b-38e8-9098-1f49e113a43f",
                        "entry_id": "d8d97704-024a-3117-a73a-608c16a75928",
                        "glosses": [
                            "An ill-tempered woman."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "dated",
                            "derogatory",
                            "literary"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "d8ac148f-30fe-473f-8d64-dc65142d9da4",
                                "sense_id": "259ff65e-ea0b-38e8-9098-1f49e113a43f",
                                "text": "Be ſhe as foule as was Florentius Loue, / As old as Sibell, and as curſt and ſhrow'd / As Socrates Zentippe, or a worſe: / She moues me not, or not remoues at leaſt / Affections edge in me."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "723b73e5-6833-4107-9005-48bbfa534dac",
                                "sense_id": "259ff65e-ea0b-38e8-9098-1f49e113a43f",
                                "text": "RICHARD HOOKER, that rare and admirable Theologiſt, [...] married a clowniſh ſilly Woman and withal a meer Xantippe, [...]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e67eaff1-cad0-4707-ac7a-13729ccec974",
                                "sense_id": "259ff65e-ea0b-38e8-9098-1f49e113a43f",
                                "text": "[page 235] He was prudent and induſtrious, and ſo good a Huſbandman, that he might have led a very eaſy and comfortable Life, had not an errant Vixen of a Wife ſoured his domeſtic Quiet. [...] [page 236] By this Xantippe (ſo was the Wife of Socrates called, ſaid Partridge) By this Xantippe he had two Sons, of which I was the younger."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f05a575a-ffe3-45ad-a44f-84a996d79aba",
                                "sense_id": "259ff65e-ea0b-38e8-9098-1f49e113a43f",
                                "text": "Who has not seen the cross looks and peevish temper of the teacher and parent copied, as by a mirror (though we should say without reflection), in the face and disposition of the child? [...] From an unbroken course of such treatment, who would expect any thing but an unbroken line of Nabals and Xanthippes?"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "459b2ed5-c7ba-4be3-bd6f-54625977fcd9",
                                "sense_id": "259ff65e-ea0b-38e8-9098-1f49e113a43f",
                                "text": "What have we seen in our own personal walks through life to make us believe that women are devils? There may possibly have been a Xantippe here and there, but Imogenes are to be found under every bush."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e0b53ade-9491-4095-902b-cb3965f923bb",
                                "sense_id": "259ff65e-ea0b-38e8-9098-1f49e113a43f",
                                "text": "The use he [Socrates] made of his domestic trial may be profitably remembered by any that have a Xanthippe to deal with."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "bd0173de-8a20-401b-bb25-4f3c14c0e44a",
                                "sense_id": "259ff65e-ea0b-38e8-9098-1f49e113a43f",
                                "text": "The 'kneeling punishment' (罰跪) would seem to be a well recognized mode of enforcing their authority, in use by Chinese Xanthippes, for the proverb says: 'The Henpecked man is obliged to kneel with a lamp on his head [to make it certain that he does not stir] until the morning watch.'"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
